  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nHigh pressure shifts to the east of the region tonight and will\nsettle over the western Atlantic into early next week. This pattern\nwill set the stage for a gradual warming trend into midweek. Each\nday, humidity values slowly climb, presenting the opportunity for\ngrowing heat stress into early September. \n\nBefore the warmest temperatures arrive, a weak upper level trough\nwill be present over southeast Canada, stretching southwestward into\nthe Great Lakes. Within the trough, a few weak systems will move \nthrough the Great Lakes into early next week. Confidence remains high\nthat the majority of the area sees dry conditions, however, there is\nstill a potential for periods of sprinkles along and north of the \nI-70 corridor. For this update, the forecast is dry. \n\nTuesday into Wednesday, a deep ridge builds into the Ohio Valley,\nwith impressive 594dm heights encroaching over the local area. If\nthis heat dome verifies, it would reach above the 95th percentile for\nheight anomalies for early September. Whenever these higher values\noccur aloft, the surface temperatures tend to respond. Low to mid 90s\nare to be expected from Monday through Wednesday. Confidence in how \nlong the ridge holds decreases late in the week as a trough over the \nGreat Lakes tries to shunt the ridge to the southwest. Temperatures \nbegin to decrease late in the week and into the weekend as the trough\nmoves into the Northeast US. Thursday is currently the best chance \nfor the next decent opportunity for rainfall and thunderstorms. \n\nFor now, heat headlines appear likely, especially as surface moisture\nrebounds through the early week and into the peak of the highest\ntemperatures on Wednesday.",
